          {
            "id": "/mycc.md/modes-team",
            "text": "**normal mode / plan mode:** mycc starts in normal mode (explore + make changes). plan mode is enabled by `/mode plan` or the `plan_on` tool; in plan mode only explorational tools are allowed and the system prompt differs.\n\n**team mode:** not actually a mode, but a state where teammates are spawned to help the lead. Orthogonal to normal/plan (solo-normal, solo-plan, team-normal, team-plan). Uses a dedicated system prompt for collaboration challenges.\n\n**lead and teammates:** the **Lead** is the main agent process (user interaction, spawns teammates, coordinates). **Teammates** are child processes executing assigned tasks. Architecture: `Terminal -> Coordinator -> Lead -> Teammates`. Teammates cannot use `tm_create`, `tm_remove`, `tm_await`, or `broadcast` directly (must request via mail to lead). Key operations (`src/context/parent/team.ts`): `createTeammate`, `listTeammates`, `awaitTeam`, `mailTo`, `broadcast` (lead only).\n\n**lead trusting teammate's autonomous cycle:** A teammate runs its own loop; two normal behaviors are NOT failures the lead should \"fix\": (1) **idle after a phase is expected, not stuck** — when a teammate finishes a phase (no open todos, no pending tool calls) it mails \"phase completed\" and enters `idle` (polls for mail/claimable issues every `POLL_INTERVAL`, `src/context/teammate-worker.ts` `enterIdleState`); the lead must not send nag mails nor take over the work. (2) **todo management is the teammate's internal affair** — it does not affect its ability to do assigned work, and the lead cannot manage a teammate's todos; do not instruct teammates to \"skip todos\" and do not treat a \"no active todos\" report as a problem. The lead should intervene only on a real stall (no output past the deadline, or a guidance request that genuinely blocks), a timeout, or an error — not on normal idle or internal todo state. A guidance-request mail is worded \"could benefit from direction\" (not necessarily blocked).\n\n**remote peer mail (cross-instance `mail_to`):** `mail_to(name=\"<session-id>/lead\", ...)` works unchanged whether the target is a **same-machine peer** (another mycc instance on THIS machine, discovered via `~/.mycc-store/discovery`) or a **remote peer** (an instance on ANOTHER machine, wired via `peer_connect`). The `mail_to` tool (`src/tools/mail_to.ts`) recognizes the `<uuid>/lead` identity pattern (session-id is a UUID, agent-name is `lead`), fail-fast validates the peer is fresh/online (`ctx.peer.isFresh`), and routes through `ctx.peer.sendPeerMail`. `PeerManager.sendPeerMail` (`src/peer/peer.ts`) is **route-aware, local-first**: (1) **LOCAL branch** — `ChannelManager.sendPeerMail` (`src/peer/channel.ts`) appends a JSONL line directly to the remote's lead mailbox (absolute path resolved from `identity.json`), gated by heartbeat freshness; the `from` field is the local identity string `<sessionId>/lead`. (2) **REMOTE branch** — when the sid is NOT a registered local identity, `sendWireMail` (`src/peer/wire-registry.ts`) resolves the pair by sid, checks the WebSocket is OPEN, stamps a high-entropy wire mail id (`<sender-sid>-<seq>-<rand>`), enforces a 4MB sender-side byte cap (`WIRE_MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES`, CJK-aware — measures serialized UTF-8 bytes not chars), and sends a `MailFrame` over the wire. The receiver's WS handler appends to its OWN mailbox via `MailBox.appendMail` (the single mailbox writer — never a hand-rolled writer), with **seen-id dedupe** (`seenMailIdRegister`, bounded LRU of 4096) so a convergence-window mail arriving on BOTH sockets of a pair is delivered once. **At-most-once:** a send failure is LOSS (no offline queue, no ack layer) — the sending agent re-issues. **Identity string:** every instance's peer identity is `<sessionId>/lead` (`IdentityManager.getIdentityString`); this is what `mail_to` accepts as `name` and what appears as `from` on received mail. COLLECT collects peer mail into a `MAIL` note the same way as teammate mail (`collectMailsAndInput`, step 2). See `docs/peers/remote-peer-protocol.md` for the full dialer/acceptor/pair-dedupe design.\n\n**todo and issue:** **Todos** are simple task tracking items (title, status, blockedBy dependencies). **Issues** are more structured (ID, status, owner, blockedBy/blocks, comments) supporting team coordination. Hash integrity: each todo has a SHA256-based hash (first 8 hex chars) from `name|done|note`; `todo_update` requires matching hash. `pinned`/`preventStop` are deliberately NOT part of the hash, so `todo_pinning` can modify them without a `todo_update` revalidation cycle. Tools: `todo_create`, `todo_update`, `todo_pinning` (lead-only, scope `['main']`), `issue_create`, `issue_claim`, `issue_list`, `issue_close`, `issue_comment`. Slash: `/todos` (pinned show 📌), `/issues`. `todo_write` is the legacy deprecated tool.\n\n**Pinned todos and the preventStop guard:** pinned todos are protected from the auto-clear that removes completed (`done`) todos; pinning is lead-only (`todo_pinning`, scope `['main']`). A pinned todo may carry a **`preventStop` guard** — a natural-language statement about the state the work must reach. While a guard holds, the turn cannot end: `handleStop()` calls `checkPreventStop()` (`src/loop/states/stop-prevent-stop.ts`) AFTER the neglection block (ESC always wins) and BEFORE `presentResult()` / the auto-mode early return, judging ALL guards in one `forkChat` (`toolChoice='none'`, escAware-wrapped, prompt-cache preserving) that returns `[{\"id\":..,\"hash\":\"..\",\"held\":true,\"reason\":\"..\"}]`. Verdicts are matched by id+hash so a hallucinated hash cannot hold the turn; the gate never mutates the todo. A hold injects a `[STOP BLOCKED]` SYSTEM note and returns COLLECT WITHOUT `markTurnBoundary()`, so the turn legitimately continues; `TurnVars.preventStopHolds` counts consecutive holds per id and escalates from the 2nd hold (naming the count and the release call). Every failure path fails OPEN — a broken evaluator never traps the agent. Teammates are unaffected. See `docs/loop/pinned-todo-prevent-stop.md`.\n\n**user session / project session:** sessions are persisted conversation states as JSON. **Project sessions** in `.mycc/sessions/` (project-specific); **user sessions** in `~/.mycc-store/sessions/` (shared across projects). **Sealed-session principle:** a session is NEVER shared; once its mycc process exits, its files (session JSON + triologue JSONL) are sealed read-only. `/load <id>` (mid-session, empty-current-session only) or `mycc --from <id>` (cold start, also `/fork`) does NOT resume the old session — it reads it read-only, LLM re-understands the transcript into a fresh context, and continues in a BRAND NEW session. Loading the same id multiple times yields DIFFERENT new sessions (variation by re-understanding) — the basis for branching. Session structure (`src/session/types.ts`): `id`, `create_time`, `project_dir`, `lead_triologue`, `child_triologues`, `teammates`, `first_query` (bookmark title). Use `/save` to copy a project session to user dir and `/load` to branch from any sealed one.\n\n**session file vs triologue JSONL:** the **session file** (`.mycc/sessions/{uuid}/session-{uuid}.json`) holds metadata only (ID, creation time, triologue paths, teammates, first query) — created once, updated in place only by the session's own running process, sealed after exit. The **triologue JSONL** (`.mycc/sessions/{uuid}/triologue-lead-{ts}.jsonl`) is the append-only conversation log — every message appended via `onMessage` in `agent-repl.ts` with `fs.appendFileSync`; the authoritative record of agent activity. The session file tells *what sessions exist and where their logs live*; the triologue JSONL contains *what actually happened*.\n\n**slash commands:** user-initiated `/`-commands handled outside the LLM tool system (meta-operations: help, sessions, modes, knowledge bases). Registry pattern (`src/slashes/index.ts`): `slashRegistry.register`/`execute`. Built-in: `/team`, `/todos`, `/skills`, `/issues`, `/save`, `/load`, `/clear`, `/wiki`, `/domain`, `/mindmap`, `/compact`, `/mode`, `/plan`, `/help`.",
            "title": "Modes & Team",
            "summary": "**Modes & Team** covers mycc's modes, team architecture, mail, todos/issues, and sessions.\n\n**Modes.** Normal vs. plan via `/mode`, `plan_on`/`plan_off`. `src/loop/prompts/lead.ts` builds four variants (`buildSoloNormalPrompt`, `buildSoloPlanPrompt`, `buildTeamNormalPrompt`, `buildTeamPlanPrompt`) — team is a *state*, orthogonal to plan/normal.\n\n**Lead & teammates.** `TeamManager` (`src/context/parent/team.ts`) spawns child processes (`createTeammate`, `listTeammates`, `awaitTeammates`, `mailTo`, `broadcast` lead-only). Teammates run their own loop (`src/context/teammate-worker.ts`, `POLL_INTERVAL`=5s, per-turn watchdog, circuit breaker after 3 failures). `enterIdleState` after a contentful phase mails \"phase completed\" and polls — expected, not stuck; neither that nor a teammate's internal todos are lead interventions. Guidance requests are worded \"could benefit from direction\" (not blocked).\n\n**Peer mail.** `mail_to` (`src/tools/mail_to.ts`) accepts three forms; `<uuid>/lead` fail-fast checks `ctx.peer.isFresh` then `PeerManager.sendPeerMail` (`src/peer/peer.ts`) — local-first `ChannelManager`, else `sendWireMail` (`src/peer/wire-registry.ts`, 4MB cap, `seenMailIdRegister` dedupe). At-most-once.\n\n**Todos/issues.** Hash = SHA256(`name|done|note`), 8 hex; `pinned`/`preventStop`/lineage excluded so lead-only `todo_pinning` (`scope:['main']`) skips revalidation.\n\n**Pinned todos & preventStop.** CORRECTION: `preventStop` **replaced** the old `reactivate` field outright — `collect-pinned-todo.ts` is deleted, no COLLECT reactivation pass remains. `handleStop()` calls `checkPreventStop()` AFTER neglection (ESC wins) and BEFORE `presentResult()`/auto return, judging all guards in one `forkChat` (`toolChoice:'none'`, escAware, tight retry budget, caller-owned spinner) returning `[{id,hash,held,reason}]`. id+hash match buys **staleness** detection only, not anti-hallucination; the gate never mutates. A hold injects `[STOP BLOCKED]` and returns COLLECT *without* `markTurnBoundary()`; `TurnVars.preventStopHolds` escalates from hold #2. Every failure path fails OPEN. `getPreventStopTodos()` is not filtered on `done`.\n\n**Sessions.** Sealed-session principle: `/load`/`--from`/`/fork` re-understand into a NEW session. Session JSON (`src/session/types.ts`) is metadata; triologue JSONL is the append-only truth.\n\n**Slashes.** `slashRegistry` (`src/slashes/index.ts`) registers ~22 commands.",
            "level": 2,
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "/mycc.md/modes-team/tool-scope-constraints",
                "text": "| Agent Type | Available Tools |\n|------------|-----------------|\n| Lead (main) | All 49 built-in tools, except `question` (child-only) |\n| Teammate (child) | All shared tools (`['main','child']`) plus `question`. Cannot use the main-only set below. |\n\n**Main-only tools**: `mycc_title`, `read_read`, `broadcast`, `hand_over`, `plan_on`, `plan_off`, `todo_pinning`, `tm_create`, `tm_remove`, `tm_await`, `peer_list`, `peer_connect`, `peer_disconnect`.\n\n> There is no separate background (bg) agent type: `bg_create` / `bg_print` / `bg_await` / `bg_remove` are ordinary tools used by the lead and by teammates.\n\n**Background Task Tools:** the bg module (`bg_create`, `bg_print`, `bg_await`, `bg_remove`) provides non-blocking command execution. `bg_create` spawns async (returns PID); `bg_print` lists tasks or shows output for a PID; `bg_await` blocks until done (optional timeout); `bg_remove` terminates by PID. Uses `spawn()` on all platforms (Windows removes `detached:true` to capture stdout/stderr); output capped ~100KB (tail-capped); killed tracked separately from failed.\n\n**Interactive Shell / /fork Command:** `/fork` spawns a new mycc instance in a separate terminal. `/fork` (current project) or `/fork --env KEY=VALUE` (forward env vars). Linux: `gnome-terminal`/`x-terminal-emulator` with bash. Windows: `PowerShell -EncodedCommand` via `wt.exe` (`shell:true` disabled, single-quoted paths).",
                "title": "Tool Scope Constraints",
                "summary": "**Tool Scope Constraints** — Every tool is a `ToolDefinition` (`src/types.ts`) with `scope: string[]`; `ToolScope = 'main' | 'child'`. `Loader.getToolsForScope(scope)` (`src/context/shared/loader.ts`) filters the loaded map with `entry.tool.scope.includes(scope)`, over the 49-entry `builtInTools` array (`src/context/shared/registry.ts`). The Lead's `AgentStateMachine` is constructed with `'main'` (`src/loop/agent-repl.ts`); the teammate loop uses `silentLoader.getToolsForScope('child')` (`src/context/teammate-worker.ts`, loader from `src/context/child-context.ts`), which drops the `['main']`-only tools: `mycc_title`, `read_read`, `broadcast`, `hand_over`, `plan_on`, `plan_off`, `todo_pinning`, `tm_create`, `tm_remove`, `tm_await`, `peer_list`, `peer_connect`, `peer_disconnect`. Conversely `question` is `['child']` only. Runtime mirrors this: `ChildTeam` (`src/context/child/team.ts`) mails the lead for create/broadcast and throws FORBIDDEN otherwise. No `'bg'` scope is in use — `bg_create`/`bg_print`/`bg_await`/`bg_remove` are plain `['main','child']` tools over `src/context/shared/bg.ts` (spawn+unref, 100KB tail cap, `killed` ≠ `failed`). `/fork` (`src/slashes/fork.ts` + `src/utils/open-terminal.ts`) spawns `--from <sid>` in a new terminal.",
                "level": 3,
                "children": [],
                "links": [
                  {
