            "id": "/mycc.md/pitfalls",
            "text": "**Tool descriptions are for LLM awareness, not user-facing:** the `description` field is read by the LLM to understand what a tool does. It should NOT be user-facing documentation with verbose parameter explanations or step-by-step workflows. The description is about **what** the tool does, not **how** it does it internally. Implementation details (confirmation prompts, mail notifications) belong in code comments, not the description field. Keep it concise and LLM-oriented.\n\n**DeepSeek API pitfalls:** when using DeepSeek (API_PROVIDER=deepseek): `web_fetch` is unavailable; `web_search` IS available (server-side via the Responses API) and `screen`/`read_picture` ARE available (images via OpenAI-style `image_url` content blocks on /chat/completions, vision model from `DEEPSEEK_VISION_MODEL`, default `deepseek-flash`; set it to `none` to disable); DSML stripping (the letter-box strips internal DSML markup like `<ds_safety>`/`<ds_thinking>`); prompt caching (automatic for repeated prefixes); embedding still uses Ollama (wiki/RAG requires a local Ollama with `nomic-embed-text`); `tool_choice=\"none\"` for wrap-up mode (prevents raw XML tool calls). Detailed reference: `docs/providers/deepseek-api-reference.md`.\n\n**agentIO singleton works differently in child processes:** `agentIO` (`src/loop/agent-io.ts`) is imported by both main and child, but `initMain()` is only called in the main process. `isMainProcess()` returns `true` in main (lead), `false` in child (teammates) — `isMainProcessFlag` defaults to `false` and only becomes `true` after `initMain()`. **Important**: only use `isMainProcess()` to distinguish lead vs teammate context. Other methods (`ask()`, `exec()`) throw in child processes. For code working in both contexts: use `agentIO.isMainProcess()` to check, `ctx.core.question()` for user questions (IPC), `ctx.core.brief()` for logging (IPC in child).\n\n**Loop Notation (LN):** a compact notation for message role rotation in the agent loop. Each comma-separated unit is one message. Units: `system` (system prompt), `user` (user query), `[tool1, tool2]?` (assistant's batched tool call, `?` = called by LLM), `tool1!` (tool result, one at a time), `agent` (assistant text reply), `_tool1_` (tool call replaced with placeholder after compaction). Example single-turn: `system, user, [tool1]?, tool1!, agent`. Example with batching + compaction: `system, user, [tool1]?, tool1!, [tool2, tool3]?, tool2!, _tool3_, agent, user, agent` (system → user → tool1 call → result → tool2+tool3 batched → tool2 result → tool3 compacted → agent reply → user → agent reply). Conventions: `[]?` groups batched calls from the same delta; `!` marks a result; `_` wraps a compacted call; bare units (system/user/agent) are pure text.",
            "title": "Pitfalls",
            "summary": "The Pitfalls section (MYCC.md line 227) collects four verified warnings for agents modifying this repo:\n\n1. **Tool `description` is LLM-facing, not user docs** — say *what* a tool does, not its workflow or internals (confirmation prompts, mail notifications belong in code comments). The model pattern is `src/tools/web_fetch.ts:11`.\n\n2. **DeepSeek gaps** (`API_PROVIDER=deepseek`): `webFetch()` throws `webFetch not supported by DeepSeek provider` (`src/engine/deepseek.ts:784`); `web_search` works server-side via `POST /responses`; `screen`/`read_picture` work through `image_url` blocks with `DEEPSEEK_VISION_MODEL` (default `deepseek-flash`, `none` disables — `src/config.ts:762`); embeddings stay on Ollama (`nomic-embed-text`); DSML markup stripped by `stripInternalMarkup()` (`src/utils/letter-box.ts:55`); wrap-up uses `toolChoice:'none'` (`src/loop/esc-wrap-up.ts:73`). See `docs/providers/deepseek-api-reference.md` / `-pitfalls.md`.\n\n3. **`agentIO` child processes**: `isMainProcessFlag` starts `false`; `initMain()` flips it only in the lead. `ask()`/`exec()` throw — use `ctx.core.question()`/`brief()`.\n\n4. **Loop Notation (LN)**: `system, user, [tool1, tool2]?, tool1!, _tool2_, agent` — `[]?` = batched LLM call, `!` = result, `_x_` = compacted. Source: `docs/loop/esc-wrap-up-redesign.md`.",
            "level": 2,
            "children": [],
            "links": [
              {
                "target_type": "file",
                "file_path": "src/tools/web_fetch.ts",
                "comment": "Line 11 shows the concise LLM-oriented `description` pattern the Pitfalls section prescribes."
              },
              {
                "target_type": "term",
                "term_name": "Loop Notation (LN)",
                "comment": "Compact message-role notation for the agent loop (system, user, [tool]?, tool!, agent, _tool_); defined in docs/loop/esc-wrap-up-redesign.md."
              },
              {
                "target_type": "term",
                "term_name": "isMainProcessFlag",
                "comment": "Private field on AgentIO defaulting to false; only initMain() (main process) sets it true — the basis for lead-vs-teammate detection."
              },
              {
                "target_type": "term",
                "term_name": "stripInternalMarkup()",
                "comment": "letter-box.ts helper that removes DeepSeek DSML markup (<ds_safety>/<ds_thinking>) before display."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "/mycc.md/design-rationale-history",
            "text": "The \"why it is like this\" record. Source comments state the **invariant**; the\nstory of how the invariant was discovered lives here, so a file never has to be\nread as a changelog.\n\n**Triologue: no message merging.** `triologue.note()` / `user()` used to merge a\nnew note into the preceding message when the role matched. That made one\ntriologue message stand for several facts, so compaction could drop half of them\nand TP repair could not tell which. Every note is now its own message; the cost\nis a longer transcript, the benefit is a transcript that can be reasoned about\nmessage by message. `TriologueLite` (child side) still merges and therefore still\nhas the old failure mode.\n\n**Wire registry: three indexes, one live socket per sid.** A remote peer used to\nbe keyed by endpoint alone. A simultaneous mutual dial keys the same peer twice\n(our typed URL vs its announced endpoint), and a peer restart changes its sid\nunder a stable endpoint — so `pairs` (endpoint → entry), `sidIndex`\n(sid → endpoint) and `socketsBySid` (sid → socket census) now coexist. Rules that\nfall out: `sidIndex` may point at a *surviving* endpoint after a teardown\n(never delete it blindly), the census is the authority for \"who still holds this\nsid\", and a teardown must close the sockets it removes or they keep delivering\nmail from an entry nobody lists.\n\n**Peer wire: a close code is not an HTTP status.** An unauthorized upgrade is\nrefused with a bare `HTTP/1.1 401` at the upgrade handshake, which never becomes\na WS close frame — the dialer observes 1006. Any diagnosable auth failure must\ntherefore be reported by the *acceptor* (brief, not verbose), and the dialer\nmust treat an unrecorded socket close as a settled failure rather than waiting\nfor a pair that will never exist.\n\n**Teammate lifecycle: status follows the process, not the request.** Removing a\nteammate is a request; its exit is a fact. Status is written by the exit/error\nhandler (`failed` for a non-zero exit, `shutdown` for a clean one), the process\nmap entry stays until that fires so late IPC responses are not dropped, and the\nissue board is released at removal — a claimed issue with a dead owner is\nunclaimable forever, because claiming accepts draft/pending only.\n\n**Agent loop: one owner per cross-turn flag.** `crossroadOccurred`,\n`hasSeededFirstQuery`, the skill-suggester throttle and `wrapUpState` were each\nreset from several states. A flag cleared by two states is a flag whose lifetime\nnobody owns; each now has one writer, and any skip it causes is reported (a\nsilent skip is indistinguishable from \"checked, nothing there\").\n\n**Plan mode gates commands, not verbs.** The declared intent verb is written by\nthe agent being gated, so it cannot be the gate. Plan mode checks the command's\nshape (dangerous list, batch delete, write verbs, git writers) before honouring a\nread-only verb.",
            "title": "Design Rationale & History",
            "summary": "This section is the \"why it is like this\" companion to source comments: code states the invariant, this narrative records how it was discovered. It is already drafted in `MYCC.md` (Design Rationale & History, ~line 243) and is cross-referenced from `src/peer/wire-registry.ts`, whose header says the reasoning for its invariants \"lives in MYCC.md → Wire registry.\"\n\nSix stories, each backed by a source module: (1) Triologue no message merging — `src/loop/triologue.ts` holds the one-note-one-message invariant (line ~442) and deferred-input replay; `src/loop/triologue-lite.ts` still emits `merge` pieces, retaining the old failure mode. (2) Wire registry three indexes — `pairs`/`sidIndex`/`socketsBySid`, census authority, teardown closes the sockets it removes (`src/peer/wire-registry.ts`). (3) Close code ≠ HTTP status — a refused upgrade surfaces as 1006, so the acceptor (`src/serve/peer-wire.ts`, `briefPeerWireRefusal`) must report auth failures (`src/peer/wire-client.ts`). (4) Teammate status follows the process, not the request — exit handler writes `failed`/`shutdown`, `releaseIssuesOwnedBy` at removal (`src/context/parent/team.ts`). (5) One owner per cross-turn flag — `crossroadOccurred`, `hasSeededFirstQuery`, `SkillSuggester.lastQuery/cooldown` (`src/loop/states/collect-skill.ts`), `wrapUpState` (`src/loop/wrap-up-state.ts`). (6) Plan mode gates command shape, not the declared verb (`planModeCommandViolation` in `src/context/grant/bash-judge.ts`).",
            "level": 2,
            "children": [],
            "links": [
              {
                "target_type": "file",
